WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.680
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:12.120
Today you are going to see glimpses of the future&nbsp;
many of which come from the amazing AI research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:18.660
papers that appeared not so long ago. First,&nbsp;
NVIDIA just showcased their new system which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:25.980
can automatically come up with dialogue for&nbsp;
a virtual character in a video game. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:33.780
the text what the character needs to say will be&nbsp;
synthesized by an AI, then a text to voice AI will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:40.920
make it actually say it in a human voice, and the&nbsp;
mouth movements will also be synthesized. Look.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:59.400
It sounds a little stiff, could be a bit&nbsp;
more natural, but this is only the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:06.060
version of something that was previously nearly&nbsp;
impossible. And now, here it is, and it may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:13.140
part of the video games and perhaps even the&nbsp;
virtual assistants of the future. This can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:20.580
combined with Unreal Engine 5 and Metahuman, which&nbsp;
is an amazing system for creating virtual humans.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:29.100
And, look at this. What is this? This is&nbsp;
NVIDIA’s Neuralangelo, this performs something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:34.260
that researchers call photogrammetric neural&nbsp;
surface reconstruction. What does that mean? It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:41.160
means that you can scan your environment with your&nbsp;
phone camera, and out comes a 3D mesh that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:49.140
use to create a virtual world for video games, or&nbsp;
have a videoconference that actually takes place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:56.580
in this environment. Not just a 2D image, but&nbsp;
actually being there. Previous techniques really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:03.240
struggled with this. Look, here is an input scene&nbsp;
with a screw clamp. This is the Neural Implicit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:09.960
Surfaces paper from two years ago, and as you&nbsp;
see, it was unable to reconstruct the details&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:16.740
on the screw. A more recent technique, NeuralWarp&nbsp;
from just one year ago provides smoother results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:24.300
that much is clear, however the high-frequency&nbsp;
details are unfortunately still lost. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:31.980
with Neuralangelo, look. Wow. Now that is what&nbsp;
I call a high-quality reconstruction. Screws&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:38.580
finally looks like screws! And the paper reveals&nbsp;
that this particular scene was not just a fluke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:45.060
the new technique is so much better than the&nbsp;
previous ones on a number of different scenes. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:50.520
high-fidelity neural reconstruction is&nbsp;
becoming more and more of a possibility today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:56.160
Loving it. We will be able to create&nbsp;
a digital version of any place on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:02.100
planet and play with our friends there.&nbsp;
How cool is that? What a time to be alive!

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:10.860
Okay, so generating speech is a possibility, and&nbsp;
creating amazing virtual spaces is a possibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:18.060
too. However, what about animation? Here is&nbsp;
an amazingly fun paper that I would love to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:26.040
show to you. And it is about simulating locomotion&nbsp;
for virtual gibbons. This is an AI-based method&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:34.560
which can jump from vine to vine. Wait a second,&nbsp;
this can’t do anything. Well, yes, but this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:41.100
just the initial part of the training. It doesn’t&nbsp;
quite know how to do this yet. Note that to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:47.220
best of my knowledge, some of this is footage&nbsp;
that is exclusive to us, so you can only see it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:53.940
here on Two Minute Papers. Nowhere else. But this&nbsp;
doesn’t seem to work too well, does it. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.940 --> 00:04:01.320
this is a SIGGRAPH paper, so you Fellow Scholars&nbsp;
immediately know that this has to be real good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:09.180
So, you know what? Let’s wait an hour for it&nbsp;
to learn. Now, an hour later. My goodness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:17.520
look at that! It can now jump from vine to vine,&nbsp;
and hold on to your papers, because it gets even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:24.960
better. Look. It now understands that if it gets&nbsp;
a couple more swings before jumping, it can even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:31.860
do this. Wow. That is incredible. Make sure to&nbsp;
have a look at the paper in the video description,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:38.280
it is a ton of fun. And I would like to send a&nbsp;
huge thank you to the scientists at the University&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:44.640
of British Columbia for also publishing the source&nbsp;
code of this project for all of us for free.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:51.480
So, as you see, AI research is progressing&nbsp;
so incredibly fast I may have to showcase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:58.500
these works not one, but three per video to&nbsp;
be able to keep up. What do you think? Did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:03.960
you enjoy this? Let me know in the comments&nbsp;
